1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,060
CloudCart wants a new feature: a wish list.

2
00:00:03,610 --> 00:00:06,490
One feature, but six kinds of people will touch it.

3
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,024
Each one cares about something different.

4
00:00:09,574 --> 00:00:11,771
Product asks: is this worth building?

5
00:00:12,321 --> 00:00:14,391
Developers ask: how do we build it?

6
00:00:14,941 --> 00:00:19,485
QA, security, operations, and DevOps each ask their own question.

7
00:00:20,035 --> 00:00:22,595
Today you learn who does what, and why it matters.

8
00:00:24,645 --> 00:00:25,968
Why do roles matter?

9
00:00:26,518 --> 00:00:28,246
Clear roles mean clear ownership.

10
00:00:28,796 --> 00:00:32,188
Without them, important work is done twice, or not at all.

11
00:00:32,738 --> 00:00:35,767
In a small team, one person may wear several hats.

12
00:00:36,317 --> 00:00:40,178
But the responsibilities still exist, and someone must own each one.

13
00:00:42,228 --> 00:00:44,425
Product decides what to build, and why.

14
00:00:44,975 --> 00:00:47,621
They listen to customers, and set priorities.

15
00:00:48,171 --> 00:00:50,624
They keep a backlog: an ordered list of work.

16
00:00:51,174 --> 00:00:53,138
And they describe what done means,

17
00:00:53,138 --> 00:00:54,236
using requirements,

18
00:00:54,236 --> 00:00:56,721
like the ones from the requirements lesson.

19
00:00:57,271 --> 00:00:59,447
Their question: is this worth building?

20
00:01:01,497 --> 00:01:03,118
Developers build the software.

21
00:01:03,668 --> 00:01:06,484
They write the code, and review each other's changes.

22
00:01:07,034 --> 00:01:10,128
They fix bugs, and write unit tests for their own code.

23
00:01:10,678 --> 00:01:13,451
In many teams, they also help run what they build.

24
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:16,177
Their question: how do we build this well?

25
00:01:18,227 --> 00:01:20,979
QA, or quality assurance, protects quality.

26
00:01:21,529 --> 00:01:24,878
They plan tests, and run them, by hand and by automation.

27
00:01:25,428 --> 00:01:27,604
They find defects before customers do.

28
00:01:28,154 --> 00:01:32,058
Good QA also improves the process, so fewer defects appear.

29
00:01:32,608 --> 00:01:36,363
Quality is a team responsibility, not only QA's job.

30
00:01:36,913 --> 00:01:39,601
Their question: does it really work, in every case?

31
00:01:41,651 --> 00:01:44,168
Security protects the system, and the data.

32
00:01:44,718 --> 00:01:48,089
They assess risks, and set rules for access and secrets.

33
00:01:48,639 --> 00:01:51,668
They scan code and dependencies for known weaknesses.

34
00:01:52,218 --> 00:01:56,016
The modern goal is to build security in early, not add it at the end.

35
00:01:56,566 --> 00:01:58,870
Security is also everyone's job.

36
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:02,342
Their question: what could go wrong, and who could abuse it?

37
00:02:04,392 --> 00:02:06,547
Operations runs the live system.

38
00:02:07,097 --> 00:02:10,723
They monitor health, respond to incidents, and manage capacity.

39
00:02:11,273 --> 00:02:13,300
They keep backups, and apply updates.

40
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:16,175
They are often the first to know when something breaks.

41
00:02:16,725 --> 00:02:19,520
Their question: is it healthy, and can it keep running?

42
00:02:21,570 --> 00:02:23,234
DevOps connects all of this.

43
00:02:23,784 --> 00:02:27,304
A DevOps engineer builds and improves the delivery path.

44
00:02:27,854 --> 00:02:30,905
That means pipelines, automation, and environments.

45
00:02:31,455 --> 00:02:35,337
They help developers ship safely, and operations run reliably.

46
00:02:35,887 --> 00:02:40,133
DevOps is also a culture: shared goals, and shared responsibility.

47
00:02:40,683 --> 00:02:43,584
Their question: how do we deliver faster, and safer?

48
00:02:45,634 --> 00:02:47,554
Look at how many teams used to work.

49
00:02:48,104 --> 00:02:51,944
Developers wrote code, then threw it over a wall to operations.

50
00:02:52,494 --> 00:02:56,035
Developers wanted change. Operations wanted stability.

51
00:02:56,585 --> 00:02:59,167
When something broke, each side blamed the other.

52
00:02:59,717 --> 00:03:03,215
DevOps tears down the wall, with shared goals, and shared tools.

53
00:03:03,765 --> 00:03:06,603
Everyone cares about both speed, and stability.

54
00:03:08,653 --> 00:03:10,978
Here are the six roles, side by side.

55
00:03:11,528 --> 00:03:12,915
Product: what, and why.

56
00:03:13,465 --> 00:03:14,830
Developers: build.

57
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:16,596
QA: verify.

58
00:03:17,146 --> 00:03:18,597
Security: protect.

59
00:03:19,147 --> 00:03:20,640
Operations: run.

60
00:03:21,190 --> 00:03:23,132
DevOps: connect, and automate.

61
00:03:25,182 --> 00:03:27,294
Follow the wish list feature through the team.

62
00:03:27,844 --> 00:03:31,919
Product writes the story: customers can save products to a wish list.

63
00:03:32,469 --> 00:03:34,837
Developers build it, and add unit tests.

64
00:03:35,387 --> 00:03:37,157
QA tests it, in every case.

65
00:03:37,707 --> 00:03:41,889
Security checks that one customer cannot see another customer's list.

66
00:03:42,439 --> 00:03:44,871
A DevOps pipeline builds and deploys it.

67
00:03:45,421 --> 00:03:48,535
Operations monitors it, and watches for problems.

68
00:03:49,085 --> 00:03:50,877
Every role touched one feature.

69
00:03:52,927 --> 00:03:55,210
Now try it: who owns each problem?

70
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:57,829
What should we build next? Product.

71
00:03:58,379 --> 00:04:03,136
Checkout is slow at two in the morning? Operations responds, with the developers.

72
00:04:03,686 --> 00:04:08,913
A library has a known vulnerability? Security flags it, and developers upgrade it.

73
00:04:09,463 --> 00:04:13,431
Deploying takes two hours of manual steps? DevOps automates it.

74
00:04:13,981 --> 00:04:18,717
Does the discount code work in every case? QA tests it, and developers fix it.

75
00:04:19,267 --> 00:04:22,360
Notice that many problems involve more than one role.

76
00:04:24,410 --> 00:04:26,266
Watch out for four common myths.

77
00:04:26,816 --> 00:04:31,084
Myth one: a DevOps engineer is a renamed system administrator.

78
00:04:31,084 --> 00:04:31,635
In fact,

79
00:04:31,635 --> 00:04:35,008
the work centers on automation and collaboration.

80
00:04:35,558 --> 00:04:39,505
Myth two: only QA owns quality. In fact, the whole team does.

81
00:04:40,055 --> 00:04:45,729
Myth three: security slows teams down. Late security does. Early security saves time.

82
00:04:46,279 --> 00:04:49,535
Myth four: developers never need to know operations.

83
00:04:49,535 --> 00:04:52,039
Knowing how code runs makes better code.

84
00:04:52,589 --> 00:04:54,680
Roles differ, but the goal is shared.

85
00:04:56,730 --> 00:04:57,605
Quick check.

86
00:04:58,155 --> 00:05:00,437
Which role decides what to build, and why?

87
00:05:00,987 --> 00:05:02,566
Who runs the live system?

88
00:05:03,116 --> 00:05:06,743
What problem did the wall between developers and operations cause?

89
00:05:07,293 --> 00:05:09,831
Name two things a DevOps engineer builds.

90
00:05:10,381 --> 00:05:14,392
Objective covered: MC01-O05.

91
00:05:14,942 --> 00:05:17,011
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

92
00:05:23,561 --> 00:05:25,673
Next lesson: tracing one change.

93
00:05:26,223 --> 00:05:28,101
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
